  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 104 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nRain Thursday\n\n17z WV satellite shows an upper low over the Four Corners, a plume \nof moisture ahead of this feature over the southern Plains, and an \nupper trough advancing southeast over Alberta. At the surface, while \nthe region is post-frontal it is not devoid of low level moisture, \nas evidenced by extensive stratus.\n\nFor Thursday, mid level moisture plume will advect over the region. \nWith the low level moisture expected to largely remain in place, \nthis should lead to rain as forcing for ascent occurs both with a \nlead shortwave during the morning and widespread QG ascent occurs as \nthe Four Corners upper low and Alberta trough phase into one large \nscale trough that will move southeast over the Great Lakes. While \nenough moisture will be present for rain across the CWA Thursday, \nbest 700/850mb moisture transport looks to remain south/southeast of \nour forecast area. Indeed, 14.12z HREF and REFS maximum QPF values \nare under 2\" and probability-matched means are around 0.75-1\" across \nthe entire rain period. Therefore, am not expecting impacts from \nthis rainfall.\n\nPotentially windy Friday\n\nFriday, in the wake of Thursday's upper trough, ridge will build \nacross the Plains. With a strong surface pressure gradient present \nand 35-40 kt winds at 850/925mb, expect windy conditions during the \nday. A Wind Advisory may be needed down the road as 14.13z NBM daily \nmax gust probabilities at the RST/LSE airports are 55-60% to meet or \nexceed 45 mph.\n\nFrost/freeze potential Saturday morning\n\nSetup for tumbling temperatures Saturday morning remains in place \nwith the aforementioned surface high becoming centered in Kansas. \nThe northern extent of this high looks to extend northeastward into \nthe forecast area leading to light winds at the surface. With skies \nclearing as large scale descent occurs in the wake of the upper \ntrough, excellent radiational cooling conditions are likely to \nresult. Temperatures are highly likely (85-100%) to drop to levels \nsupporting frost, likely (55-100%) to dip below freezing, and \npotentially below 28 for a killing freeze, especially (60-85%) along \nand north of an Osseo-Sparta-Wisconsin Rapids arc.",
